support  the  service  providers

00:14:05.289 --> 00:14:07.930
that  this  needs  to  be  part  of  the  order,

00:14:08.089 --> 00:14:13.150
that  this  needs  to  be  in  the  language
of  the  service  provider.  Right.

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:16.730
But  there's  more.  There's
even  more  problems  coming.

00:14:16.869 --> 00:14:20.910
E-E vidence  is  just  within  the  EU.  But
there's  also  the  EU  Cybercrime  Convention,

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:25.690
also  called  the  Budapest  Convention,
which  is  actually  older.  It's  from  2001.

00:14:26.829 --> 00:14:29.509
And  81  countries  have
subscribed  to  this  already

00:14:29.509 --> 00:14:33.289
and  a  further  16  are  invited  to  exceed

00:14:33.289 --> 00:14:38.650
and  will  be  part  of  the  Budapest  Convention
soon.  So  there's  many  EU  countries

00:14:38.650 --> 00:14:42.690
with  vastly  different  legal  systems.
The  way  the  evidence  was  argued

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:49.250
is  the  protections  come  from  us  basically,
or  within  the  EU  basically  having

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:53.589
at  least  a  common  understanding
on  how  protections  work.

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:57.549
We  have  the  ECJ  you  can  go  to  if  you
feel  your  rights  are  violated  and  so  on.

00:14:57.750 --> 00:15:03.349
How  does  that  work  if  there's  81  countries?
That  includes  whatever,  Japan,  the  US,

00:15:03.529 --> 00:15:09.230
but  also  countries  we  might  feel
questionable  nowadays,  Georgia,  Hungary,

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:14.269
a  lot  of  countries  where  we  might  not
feel  safe  anymore,  just  releasing  data  to.

00:15:15.369 --> 00:15:18.589
But  under  the  second  additional
protocol  of  Budapest  Convention,

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:24.349
there's  also  these  changes  now,  and  you
can  also  go  directly  to  service  providers

00:15:24.349 --> 00:15:28.970
and  ask  them  for  data.  So  law
enforcement  from  all  these  countries  can,

00:15:29.309 --> 00:15:31.589
once  it's  enacted,  it's
not  enacted  right  now,

00:15:32.170 --> 00:15:35.950
it  will  probably  be  enacted
early ' 27  or  mid -'27.

00:15:37.509 --> 00:15:41.170
Then  from  all  the  signatory
countries  of  the  second  protocol,

00:15:41.309 --> 00:15:44.750
which  is  currently  51,  they  can
then  also  go  to  service  providers

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:47.529
and  directly  ask  for  data.
And  it's  even  broader  scope,

00:15:47.529 --> 00:15:51.089
there  could  be  financial  data,
administrative  services  and  so  on,

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:55.549
so  they  can  go  to  your  tax
auditor  and  things  like  that.

00:15:57.430 --> 00:16:00.210
And  requests  might  even  be
for  IP  rights  violations,

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:01.789
which  is  a  big  problem,  obviously.

00:16:03.849 --> 00:16:07.049
But  there  is  no  provision  for
a  central  database  at  all.

00:16:07.569 --> 00:16:09.750
So  neither  is  it  clear  how  law  enforcement

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:11.430
will  actually  find  the
right  service  provider

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:13.690
and  not  address  the  question
to  the  wrong  service  provider,

00:16:13.690 --> 00:16:15.269
which  might  have  a  similar  name.

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:18.750
But  it's  also  unclear  on
how  can  service  providers

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:22.329
actually  verify  that  this  is  a
legitimate  law  enforcement  agency

00:16:22.329 --> 00:16:24.650
or  legitimate  judge's  signature
or  something  like  that.

00:16:24.650 --> 00:16:29.349
If  there  is  no  database,  no  way  to
verify  it,  how  can  we  validate  signatures?

00:16:29.349 --> 00:16:36.289
If  there  is  no  database  and  how  to
do  this.  And  also  a  contested  issue  is

00:16:36.289 --> 00:16:42.009
how  do  we  protect  response  data?
Some  countries,  especially  from  Africa,

00:16:42.369 --> 00:16:45.730
feel  that  it  should  be  sent  as
is,  it's  not  encrypted,  nothing.

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:51.869
And  obviously  this  is  prohibited  by
GDPR  for  European  service  providers.

00:16:51.869 --> 00:16:56.869
We  just  can't  send  data  unencrypted,
which  is  pertaining  to  private  individuals.

00:16:58.769 --> 00:17:02.130
Upcoming,  just  for  completeness,
is  UN  Cybercrime  Convention,

00:17:02.170 --> 00:17:07.269
which  was  basically  brought  up  because
Russia,  China,  Iran  and  these  countries

00:17:07.269 --> 00:17:09.230
were  not  allowed  to
join  Budapest  Convention

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:16.690
because  their  systems  are  deemed  to  be
incompatible  with  the  fundamental  ideas

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:21.609
and  human  rights  protections  of  Budapest
Convention.  So  let's  see  where  that  goes.

00:17:22.150 --> 00:17:26.289
I  haven't  seen  anything  on  the  implementation
of  the  service  providers  so  far.

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:35.970
Unfortunately,  in  2024,  we  lost  a  significant
battle  with  the  ECJ  HADOPI  ruling,

00:17:36.250 --> 00:17:44.329
which  is  a  big  problem  because  what  the
ECJ  said  is  that  they  basically  separated

00:17:45.309 --> 00:17:54.789
IP  address  storage  from  service
storage.  And  that  initiated  a  new  push

00:17:54.789 --> 00:17:56.329
for  data  retention  throughout  Europe.

00:17:56.750 --> 00:18:01.890
A  lot  of  countries  and  legislators  now
thought,  oh,  there  is  a  way  we  can  do  this.

00:18:01.950 --> 00:18:04.029
We  can  actually  have  IP  address  retention

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:09.410
and  we  can  use  it  for
all  sorts  of  crimes,

00:18:09.430 --> 00:18:15.109
not  just  for  serious  crime  on  national
security.  And  that  was  supported,

00:18:15.170 --> 00:18:19.609
unfortunately  the  whole  idea  was  supported
by  von der Leyen's   political  guidance

00:18:19.609 --> 00:18:23.309
which  basically  asked  for,  oh,
yes,  we  need  data  retention,

00:18:23.569 --> 00:18:27.049
but  we  will  also  safeguard  fundamental
rights  however  this  is  going  to  happen.

00:18:27.509 --> 00:18:30.650
And  there  was  a  call  for  evidence
in  a  public  consultation  in  2025.

00:18:31.109 --> 00:18:35.890
The  principal  changes  in  court  position
was  this  clear  split  of  data  retention

00:18:35.890 --> 00:18:40.650
from  the  service  users'  retention
and  that  they  explicitly  said

00:18:40.650 --> 00:18:43.009
that  the  IP  address  retention  alone

00:18:43.009 --> 00:18:49.849
is  no  longer  considered  a  serious  infringement,
which,  well,  we'll  see  about  that.

00:18:50.029 --> 00:18:53.170
But  the  limitations  are  basically,
I'm  not  going  to  recite  this,

00:18:53.309 --> 00:18:56.289
is  basically  everything  needs
to  be  separately  stored.

00:18:56.829 --> 00:19:01.630
Service  data  cannot  be  overlaid
with  IP  retained  address  data,

00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:05.410
at  least  not  by  the  service  provider,
and  that's  good  enough,  more  or  less.

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:12.930
So  what  does  IP  data  retention  in  that
context  entail?  It's  basically  nothing  new,

00:19:13.029 --> 00:19:17.009
and  most  importantly,  it's  not  a
reduced  scope  for  IT  data  retention.

00:19:19.289 --> 00:19:23.569
It  was  always  the  case  that  access
providers  needed  to  store  IP  addresses.

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:28.490
That  hasn't  changed.  With  the  timestamp
and  when  was  that  IP  address  assigned

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:33.049
and  how  long  was  it  used  and  so  on,  no
changes  there.  Increased  amount  of  data,

00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:39.509
even  increased  amount  of  data  is  now
required  with  ports  and  probably  IMSIs

00:19:39.509 --> 00:19:43.789
and  things  like  that  in  order  to  be
sure  that  with  the  data  set  given,

00:19:43.930 --> 00:19:54.650
you  can  actually  identify  the  user.  So,
right.  No  flow  data  is  to  be  stored,

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:58.130
so  you  can't  really  build  a  profile
on  this  storage.  That  is  true.

00:20:00.329 --> 00:20:05.890
But  it  is  still  more  data  than
was  supposed  to  be  stored  before.

00:20:06.089 --> 00:20:10.710
The  time  frame  has  become  longer.
I'll  talk  about  time  frames  in  a  bit.

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:17.390
So  there  is  no  reduction  in  anything.  So
basically,  IP  data  retention  has  stayed

00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:20.769
as  it  was  from  2004
onwards.  Always  the  same.

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:25.809
Other  than  for  the  service  provider
side,  we  need  to  store  it  separately.

00:20:26.009 --> 00:20:28.769
It  needs  to  be  a  safe  system.  It
needs  to  even  support  safe  deletion,

00:20:29.049 --> 00:20:35.369
whatever  that  means.  And  it's  now  also
usable  for  all  sorts  of  criminal  offenses,

00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:40.210
which  is  a  huge  problem  because  all
sorts  of  criminal  offenses  can  be  anything

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:45.849
up  until,  and  that's  obviously  the  French
position,  intellectual  property  rights.

00:20:46.269 --> 00:20:49.890
So  something  you  would  definitely
not  consider,  at  least  most  people,

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:53.890
would  not  consider  it  a  serious  crime,
but  that  is  obviously  up  for  discussion

00:20:53.890 --> 00:21:01.230
on  where  you  are.  Then  there  was  the
2025  consultation  by  the  commission.

00:21:02.430 --> 00:21:12.029
And  also  there  was  a  round  of  questions
sent  by  the  presidency  to  the  countries.

00:21:12.470 --> 00:21:17.250
So  the  commission  asked,  first
for  evidence,  basically  saying,

00:21:17.309 --> 00:21:20.650
oh,  what  can  be  done  with
retained  data  and  how  does  it  work

00:21:20.650 --> 00:21:22.950
and  what  are  the  time  frames
for  what  happens  there?

00:21:23.269 --> 00:21:27.370
And  then  there  was  a  public
consultation  which  went  until  September.

00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:32.289
So  basically  anyone  could,  and  there  were
thousands  of  submissions  by  individuals

00:21:32.289 --> 00:21:36.650
and  privacy  rights  organizations  and  so  on,

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:43.450
which  I  hate  to  say  are  mostly  dismissed.
They're  not  seriously  looking  at  it.

00:21:44.690 --> 00:21:48.750
More  interestingly,  on  November  27th,
the  presidency  offered  an  insight

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:50.070
in  what  the  member  states  submitted.

00:21:50.309 --> 00:21:54.350
So  the  15  member  states  who  actually
submitted  something,  they  basically  said,

00:21:54.410 --> 00:21:56.049
no,  no,  we  need  a  minimum  of  six  months

00:21:56.049 --> 00:22:00.070
IP  data  retention  extendable
by  national  laws.  Otherwise,

00:22:00.910 --> 00:22:02.930
they  wouldn't  be  able  to  compromise  on  it

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:05.490
because  some  countries  have  no
retention,  some  have  six  months,

00:22:05.570 --> 00:22:11.350
12  months,  up  to  six  years.  So  how  do
you  compromise?  Oh,  just  allow  that  more

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:16.930
is  permissible  as  well.  They  want  access
for  all  sorts  of  criminal  offenses.

00:22:17.430 --> 00:22:24.190
No  news  here.  And  that's  the  biggie
here.  Mandatory  service  level  retention

00:22:24.190 --> 00:22:30.210
for  all  telecommunication  OTT
services,  but  also  for  DNS,  for  VPN,

00:22:30.769 --> 00:22:37.610
for  cloud  services,  file  storage,  financial
services,  online  shops,  gaming,  hotel,

00:22:37.769 --> 00:22:41.509
taxi,  food  deliveries,  and  so  on.
So  everyone.  Everyone  who's  offering

00:22:41.509 --> 00:22:46.430
electronic  services  would  ideally  store
service  data  for  six  months  plus.

00:22:48.670 --> 00:22:52.750
So  my  personal  assessment  of  the
country's  wish  list,  and  I  call  it

00:22:52.750 --> 00:22:59.029
the  wet  dream  list,  of  course,
yeah,  but  it's,  there  was  never  any,

00:22:59.289 --> 00:23:04.630
that  more  than  four  weeks  of  data
retention  does  anything  meaningful

00:23:04.630 --> 00:23:10.570
for  actually  more  cases  being
solved  that  was  never  demonstrated.

00:23:10.850 --> 00:23:13.830
There's  no  studies  on  this.
There's  even  the  data  released

00:23:13.830 --> 00:23:16.470
by  law  enforcement  themselves.
The  statistics  tell  you

00:23:16.470 --> 00:23:19.049
there  is  no  meaningful
increase  after  four  weeks,

00:23:19.070 --> 00:23:22.630
and  most  of  the  processes  have
streamlined  in  a  way  that  this  works.

00:23:23.230 --> 00:23:27.789
There  are,  however,  a  number,
a  handful  of  high -profile  cases

00:23:27.789 --> 00:23:31.509
where  in  international,  through
international  corporations,  some  crime  rings

00:23:31.509 --> 00:23:35.210
are  being  fought  and  so  on,  and
there,  it  actually  takes  forever

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:40.630
to  set  up  these  proceedings,  and  so  they
might  need  data  for  longer  than  that.

00:23:41.289 --> 00:23:44.630
But  for  general  retention,  you
always  argue  about  the  mass,

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:50.110
the  high  amount  of  cases,  and  not
about  single  and  individual  cases.

00:23:50.110 --> 00:23:55.370
You'll  always  find  a  case  where
more  time  would  solve  a  single  case,

00:23:55.549 --> 00:24:00.370
even  after  years.  It  could  happen
all  the  time.  But  that  is  no  grounds

00:24:00.370 --> 00:24:05.190
for  data  retention.  And  obviously,  the,

00:24:06.350 --> 00:24:08.590
and  that's  what  law
enforcement  has  in  mind,  right?

00:24:08.670 --> 00:24:12.330
The  envisioned  combination  of  usage
data  from  all  these  sorts  of  services

00:24:12.330 --> 00:24:20.210
up  to  your  food  service,  whatever,
obviously  fails  this  clear,

00:24:24.130 --> 00:24:29.130
what  the  ECJ  basically  said  is
that  still  from  the  data,  yes,  yes,

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:33.930
IP  data  can  be  stored,  but  again,
no  profile  of  an  individual  should,

00:24:34.309 --> 00:24:38.170
you  couldn't  do  a  profile  of  an
individual  from  the  service  data

00:24:38.170 --> 00:24:43.150
stored  at  some  other  location.  And  this
clearly  fails  this  test.  If  you  use

00:24:43.150 --> 00:24:48.330
all  the  service  data,  and  then  you
can  actually  identify  who's  behind  that

00:24:48.330 --> 00:24:52.870
through  IP  address  retention,  you
still  have  mass  surveillance.  You  can,

00:24:52.870 --> 00:24:58.769
you  can  completely,  look,  you  have
the  glass  citizen,  right?  You  can  look

00:24:58.769 --> 00:25:01.590
through  everything.  And,

00:25:03.730 --> 00:25:06.690
I  mean,  this  will  be  an  uphill
battle  for  civil  society,  right?

00:25:06.830 --> 00:25:13.850
So  to  demonstrate  in  front  of  the  courts  that
several  individual  legal  provisions  now  lead

00:25:13.850 --> 00:25:16.470
to  mass  surveillance  is  kind
of  like  what  we  had  in

00:25:16.470 --> 00:25:19.430
this  Überwachungsgesamtrechnung
discussion  in  Germany.

00:25:19.590 --> 00:25:26.370
It  will  be  so  difficult  to  show  that
through  these  individual  atomized  measures,

00:25:26.590 --> 00:25:29.470
so  what  law  enforcement,  or  what  the
states  are  doing  right  now  is  more  or  less

00:25:29.470 --> 00:25:35.110
atomizing  these  individual  provisions
and  then  trying  to  overlay  it  through

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:42.029
automated  access  even  internationally.
So,  yes,  you'll  have,  law  enforcement

00:25:42.029 --> 00:25:46.230
will  have  all  the  access  they  want,
but  nationally  on  the  individual  cases,

00:25:46.230 --> 00:25:52.410
they  can  basically  claim  the  individual
measures  are  non -invasive  or  at  least

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:58.529
not  as  invasive  so  that  they're
not  permissible.  Which  leads  us

00:25:58.529 --> 00:26:03.090
to  the  reintroduction  of  data  retention
in  Germany.  We  have  the  third  iteration

00:26:03.090 --> 00:26:11.390
now  up.  The  service  providers  were  sent
the  draft  law  on  the  22nd  of  December

00:26:11.390 --> 00:26:19.650
and  we  have  for  consultation  until  the
31st  of  January.  And  it  only  calls  for  IP

00:26:19.650 --> 00:26:26.130
address  assignment  retention.  No
service  retention.  No  general  service.

00:26:26.210 --> 00:26:30.210
Not  even  for  telephony.  There's  no
service  retention  in  this  anymore.

00:26:31.150 --> 00:26:37.610
But  you  need  to  store  ports,  location,
IMSI,  MAC,  and  so  on.  But  there  is

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:43.210
something  in  there  which  is  interesting,
which  is  supposedly  only  a  transposition

00:26:43.210 --> 00:26:47.610
of  what  is  called  an  epoch  PR.  This  comes
from  E-Evidence  and  it's  a  preservation

00:26:47.610 --> 00:26:53.529
order.  So  if  law  enforcement  cannot  produce
a  proper  release  order  at  any  given

00:26:53.529 --> 00:26:57.509
point  in  time,  they  can  ask  for
preservation  of  data.  Under E -Evidence,

00:26:57.710 --> 00:27:05.650
this  is  only  data  up  until  this  point  in
time.  Not  forward -looking.  What  we  had,

00:27:05.809 --> 00:27:10.529
the  discussion  we  had  in  Germany  for
quick  freeze,  at  least  the  latest  version

00:27:10.529 --> 00:27:14.570
of  this,  was  also  forward -looking.  It
didn't  just  freeze  data,  it  was  also

00:27:14.570 --> 00:27:19.970
storing  data  for  the  future.  What  they
did  now  is  under  the  umbrella  of EPOC- PR,

00:27:20.870 --> 00:27:24.970
we're  basically  saying,  oh,  the  German
version,  we  can  do  it  forward -looking

00:27:24.970 --> 00:27:28.870
as  well.  And  forward -looking  up  until
now  always  required  a  judge's  signature

00:27:28.870 --> 00:27:32.549
because  that's  supposed  to  be  a  surveillance
measure.  Law  enforcement  can  go  to

00:27:32.549 --> 00:27:36.690
the  judge,  ask  for  it  before  they  issue
the  request.  And  they  can  basically

00:27:36.690 --> 00:27:42.570
go  to  all  service  providers  now  and
ask  for  it  and  they  don't  even  need  to

00:27:42.570 --> 00:27:46.130
demonstrate  probable  cause.  They  can  basically
go  to  the  service  provider  and  say,

00:27:46.210 --> 00:27:52.670
oh,  there's  a  risk  here,  store  it.  Do  it
for  any  time,  three  months,  six  months,

00:27:52.750 --> 00:27:59.910
it's  not  very  clear  right  now.  So,  moving
to  the  more  interesting  parts  for  this

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:06.730
audience  maybe,  the  whole  procedures  are
also  an  invite  to  side -channel  attacks.

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:12.250
So,  under  E-Evidence,  we  already  identified
some  problems.  So,  any  compromised

00:28:12.250 --> 00:28:16.090
law  enforcement  account  can  request  all
subscriber  data  from  any  service  provider

00:28:16.090 --> 00:28:22.150
within  the  EU.  It  doesn't  matter  how  it
was  compromised.  If  it's  a  compromised

00:28:22.150 --> 00:28:27.650
